(2504654, 338809, 24503, '<td valign="top"><b>Hello from Álex, CTP MkII </b> <br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b>: WOW. Very nice skyscape. And the title selected is absolutely smart.<br/><b>Composition</b>: well composed and good proportions of sky and sea. Maybe the bottom right is a bit too dark and it seems lightly unbalanced. <br/><b>Subject</b>: I think the subject is alright for the challenge.<br/><b>Technical</b>: very good in technical aspects. The colors are great except in the "dark side". Focus is OK. It makes some little jpeg artifacts, at least on my monitor.<br/><b>Improvement</b>: maybe process the dark zone to make it a little bit more detailed<br/><b>Summary</b>: really great shot. Don''t worry about scoring, because it depends on what voters expected to see in the challenge.<br/> <br/>Álex<br/></td>', 'Hello from Álex, CTP MkII   First Impression: WOW. Very nice skyscape. And the title selected is absolutely smart.Composition: well composed and good proportions of sky and sea. Maybe the bottom right is a bit too dark and it seems lightly unbalanced. Subject: I think the subject is alright for the challenge.Technical: very good in technical aspects. The colors are great except in the "dark side". Focus is OK. It makes some little jpeg artifacts, at least on my monitor.Improvement: maybe process the dark zone to make it a little bit more detailedSummary: really great shot. Don''t worry about scoring, because it depends on what voters expected to see in the challenge. Álex', '2006-06-10 12:20:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(2504756, 339467, 53147, '<td valign="top">Very cool shot. Great blue shades and the background texture gives it a nice flowing feel. Excellent pro essing to get all the black to flow niceley together. I personally prefer this to the outtake shot. Good work.</td>', 'Very cool shot. Great blue shades and the background texture gives it a nice flowing feel. Excellent pro essing to get all the black to flow niceley together. I personally prefer this to the outtake shot. Good work.', '2006-06-10 13:43:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(2504905, 340036, 15204, '<td valign="top">Hello from the critque club,<br/> <br/>A great story-telling shot here.  It works very well with the lyric that you chose to depict.<br/> <br/>I like the detail and sharpness of the whole shot.  The details of both the man and the stone wall behind him really add character to the image.  I like the vertical composition and the negative space.  It adds weight to the image and gives him a real sense of place.  LIke he is smaller in the shadow of the buildings around him.  I do like that you included the sign in the upper corner, too.  It closes off that corner and keeps my eye from leaving the frame.<br/> <br/>I agree with the commenters that it is a pity that his feet are cut off.  That happens sometimes and can''t be avoided, but it is definitely something to look out for when framing your shot.  His face is also a little shadowed, which is I am sure the way the ambient light was falling.  It would be nice to find an angle or another way to get more light on his face.<br/> <br/>Well done, here.  I like this one very much.<br/> <br/>Cheers,<br/>Liza</td>', 'Hello from the critque club, A great story-telling shot here.  It works very well with the lyric that you chose to depict. I like the detail and sharpness of the whole shot.  The details of both the man and the stone wall behind him really add character to the image.  I like the vertical composition and the negative space.  It adds weight to the image and gives him a real sense of place.  LIke he is smaller in the shadow of the buildings around him.  I do like that you included the sign in the upper corner, too.  It closes off that corner and keeps my eye from leaving the frame. I agree with the commenters that it is a pity that his feet are cut off.  That happens sometimes and can''t be avoided, but it is definitely something to look out for when framing your shot.  His face is also a little shadowed, which is I am sure the way the ambient light was falling.  It would be nice to find an angle or another way to get more light on his face. Well done, here.  I like this one very much. Cheers,Liza', '2006-06-10 15:52:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2505065, 338962, 51889, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the critiques! :)</td>', 'Thanks for all the critiques! :)', '2006-06-10 18:19:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2505558, 340419, 51802, '<td valign="top"><b>Hello from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>The first thing I notice it that the focus is off, which I am sure you are aware of already.  I also think it is cropped a little too tight.  It would be nice if some of the brick wall was visible to the right of the postcard of Lenin (I am assuming it is a post card).<br/> <br/>I really like the colors.  The bright reds and whites work very well against the weathered brick wall.  This really speaks volumes to the point that the USSR was putting tremendous effort into the facade they presented to the rest of the world while behind the scenes they were on the verge of collapse.<br/> <br/>I really think that if the focus were sharp, this would have scored a full point higher.  I did not vote in this challenge, but with sharp focus, I would have given this a 6.<br/> <br/>-Bill</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club!  The first thing I notice it that the focus is off, which I am sure you are aware of already.  I also think it is cropped a little too tight.  It would be nice if some of the brick wall was visible to the right of the postcard of Lenin (I am assuming it is a post card). I really like the colors.  The bright reds and whites work very well against the weathered brick wall.  This really speaks volumes to the point that the USSR was putting tremendous effort into the facade they presented to the rest of the world while behind the scenes they were on the verge of collapse. I really think that if the focus were sharp, this would have scored a full point higher.  I did not vote in this challenge, but with sharp focus, I would have given this a 6. -Bill', '2006-06-10 22:13:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2506264, 340578, 24503, '<td valign="top"><b>Hello from Álex, CTP MkII </b> <br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b>: wonderful hill and sky. By the way, you choosed a good title, it arrived to the first place...<br/><b>Composition</b>: very well balanced.<br/><b>Subject</b>: perfect for the challenge.<br/><b>Technical</b>: great PP of the sky and the hill. The man and the hill are too oversharpened for me (I know what I''m talking, people killed me in architecture because I oversharpened the building).<br/><b>Improvement</b>: Don''t oversharpen so much.<br/><b>Summary</b>: nice topic and place. Congrats for your 2nd personal best.<br/> <br/>Álex<br/></td>', 'Hello from Álex, CTP MkII   First Impression: wonderful hill and sky. By the way, you choosed a good title, it arrived to the first place...Composition: very well balanced.Subject: perfect for the challenge.Technical: great PP of the sky and the hill. The man and the hill are too oversharpened for me (I know what I''m talking, people killed me in architecture because I oversharpened the building).Improvement: Don''t oversharpen so much.Summary: nice topic and place. Congrats for your 2nd personal best. Álex', '2006-06-11 06:52:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(2506552, 340496, 32767, '<td valign="top">Easily the best out of the whole show. Congrats!</td>', 'Easily the best out of the whole show. Congrats!', '2006-06-11 11:43:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(2507057, 339469, 14414, '<td valign="top">I think you''ve already identified the main issues with what you''ve said in the thread but here goes:<br/> <br/><b><i>Composition </i></b> <br/>Nice to be offcenter,  looking back across to the viewer.<br/> <br/><b><i>Technical stuff (exposure, dof, lighting etc) </i></b> <br/>Focus seems a tad soft on the face, but this may be just something to do with the lighting, because if you look along the same plane focus seems ok (ie on back, dress), and shutterspeed wasnt slow enough for blur.<br/>You''re already aware of the blownout highlights, must have been difficult lighting to work with. This brings it down a lot, in terms of what I (or others I suspect) would have voted it.<br/> <br/><b><i>Meeting the challenge </i></b> <br/>Definitely, a lot of people chose that lyric, although I think I personally would have preferred a less posed photo for the quote, but then again I''m too young to really know the songs well so that may just be me.<br/> <br/><b><i>Post-processing</i></b> <br/>You''ve done as well as you could have to rescue the highlights, but sometimes you just need to get it right (with luck) in-camera. I think the spotlight works to give interest (ignoring the rules here)<br/> <br/><b><i>My personal opinion </i></b> <br/>A nice picture for her to keep, but the blownoutness ruins it in a dpc context. Sorry about the DQ though, its easy to misunderstand the rules as they are now, but at least now you know and won''t do it again</td>', 'I think you''ve already identified the main issues with what you''ve said in the thread but here goes: Composition  Nice to be offcenter,  looking back across to the viewer. Technical stuff (exposure, dof, lighting etc)  Focus seems a tad soft on the face, but this may be just something to do with the lighting, because if you look along the same plane focus seems ok (ie on back, dress), and shutterspeed wasnt slow enough for blur.You''re already aware of the blownout highlights, must have been difficult lighting to work with. This brings it down a lot, in terms of what I (or others I suspect) would have voted it. Meeting the challenge  Definitely, a lot of people chose that lyric, although I think I personally would have preferred a less posed photo for the quote, but then again I''m too young to really know the songs well so that may just be me. Post-processing You''ve done as well as you could have to rescue the highlights, but sometimes you just need to get it right (with luck) in-camera. I think the spotlight works to give interest (ignoring the rules here) My personal opinion  A nice picture for her to keep, but the blownoutness ruins it in a dpc context. Sorry about the DQ though, its easy to misunderstand the rules as they are now, but at least now you know and won''t do it again', '2006-06-11 19:15:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(2507091, 340578, 30454, '<td valign="top">Greetings from your own critique club. Sorry, I was off the track for a while.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b> <br/>Very Nice shot. <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>Very good composition.<br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>I really like the subject. Nice take on the challenge. Actually one with the same theme(song) won the blue ribbon, amazing!!<br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Color and lighting is good after the processing. <br/><b>Improvement:</b> <br/>The processing has helped the sky and the hill. But adversly affected the man. It''s either sharpening or NI gave the man plastic look. I don''t like the border. I would have processed whole picture. <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Nice image, would have scored even higher if the edges where not so sharp on the man.<br/> <br/>Over all very nice image. Congrates and Keep shooting.</td>', 'Greetings from your own critique club. Sorry, I was off the track for a while. First Impression Very Nice shot. Composition: Very good composition.Subject: I really like the subject. Nice take on the challenge. Actually one with the same theme(song) won the blue ribbon, amazing!!Technical (Colour and light): Color and lighting is good after the processing. Improvement: The processing has helped the sky and the hill. But adversly affected the man. It''s either sharpening or NI gave the man plastic look. I don''t like the border. I would have processed whole picture. Summary: Nice image, would have scored even higher if the edges where not so sharp on the man. Over all very nice image. Congrates and Keep shooting.', '2006-06-11 19:49:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2507134, 340223, 30454, '<td valign="top">Greetings from your own critique club.<br/> <br/>Once again, absolately stunning. Yanko, you are on roll. Want to share your secrete now??<br/> <br/><b>First Impression</b> <br/>Very Nice candid shot. <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>Perfect composition.<br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Very nice, meets the challenge.<br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Color and lighting is very good. Processing is very nice. <br/><b>Improvement:</b> <br/>Only thing I can think of is the improvement in the BG. Just wondering if it was possible to ake it from different angle to change the BG. <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Over all very nice porcessing and image.<br/> <br/>Cheers!!<br/></td>', 'Greetings from your own critique club. Once again, absolately stunning. Yanko, you are on roll. Want to share your secrete now?? First Impression Very Nice candid shot. Composition: Perfect composition.Subject: Very nice, meets the challenge.Technical (Colour and light): Color and lighting is very good. Processing is very nice. Improvement: Only thing I can think of is the improvement in the BG. Just wondering if it was possible to ake it from different angle to change the BG. Summary: Over all very nice porcessing and image. Cheers!!', '2006-06-11 20:23:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(2507138, 339857, 14414, '<td valign="top">Congrats on a PB and another top-20. Sorry to leave it to the last minute<br/> <br/><b><i>Composition </i></b> <br/>Because the surroundings aren''t the most exciting (ie if you''d cropped less you wouldnt have introduced new interest) you needed to fill the frame pretty much, but you''ve managed to do that without it being too centred and static.<br/> <br/><b><i>Technical stuff (exposure, dof, lighting etc) </i></b> <br/>FOcus is brilliant, especially considering the fleeting moment and the borderline shutterspeed. Exposed well for the dark birds and much lighter b/g<br/> <br/><b><i>Meeting the challenge </i></b> <br/>Without a doubt. Not only does the quote fit the photo, it helps give it extra emotion.<br/> <br/><b><i>Post-processing </i></b> <br/>Didnt think there was much to say about PP work ''til I looked at the original - you''ve very mcuh got the full potential out of the photo, much better than I would have done.<br/> <br/><b><i>Misc</i></b> <br/>I love the earthy tones. The border fits well with these. Awesome timing.<br/> <br/><b><i>My personal opinion </i></b> <br/>Great entry to the challenge, well-deserving of the score &amp; placement. Well done<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-06-11 19:25:44.</i></td>', 'Congrats on a PB and another top-20. Sorry to leave it to the last minute Composition  Because the surroundings aren''t the most exciting (ie if you''d cropped less you wouldnt have introduced new interest) you needed to fill the frame pretty much, but you''ve managed to do that without it being too centred and static. Technical stuff (exposure, dof, lighting etc)  FOcus is brilliant, especially considering the fleeting moment and the borderline shutterspeed. Exposed well for the dark birds and much lighter b/g Meeting the challenge  Without a doubt. Not only does the quote fit the photo, it helps give it extra emotion. Post-processing  Didnt think there was much to say about PP work ''til I looked at the original - you''ve very mcuh got the full potential out of the photo, much better than I would have done. Misc I love the earthy tones. The border fits well with these. Awesome timing. My personal opinion  Great entry to the challenge, well-deserving of the score & placement. Well done', '2006-06-11 20:25:27.000000', '2006-06-11 20:25:44.000000', 0),
(2508447, 340509, 58947, '<td valign="top">Wow, this is a lovely shot. Very moody. Dig it. :-)</td>', 'Wow, this is a lovely shot. Very moody. Dig it. :-)', '2006-06-12 04:19:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2509021, 340276, 43410, '<td valign="top">hello, great image, bud.  a lot of imagination and thought went into this, and it definitely fit the challenge.  with this, i believe the only thing that really counted off for you was the blownout highlight points in the middle/right portion of the image.  this was from the large aperture you used, letting in too much light.  not that it''s a bad thing.  i absolutely loved the image, and gave it a 7.  those pesky refrigerator lights are just bright, aren''t they?  but seriously, keep on shooting, you have some impressive work in your portfolio.  you''ll get better every challenge :)<br/> <br/>regards,<br/>-Jon Rowe</td>', 'hello, great image, bud.  a lot of imagination and thought went into this, and it definitely fit the challenge.  with this, i believe the only thing that really counted off for you was the blownout highlight points in the middle/right portion of the image.  this was from the large aperture you used, letting in too much light.  not that it''s a bad thing.  i absolutely loved the image, and gave it a 7.  those pesky refrigerator lights are just bright, aren''t they?  but seriously, keep on shooting, you have some impressive work in your portfolio.  you''ll get better every challenge :) regards,-Jon Rowe', '2006-06-12 11:05:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2509267, 340370, 48268, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Louison:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by TooCool:</b></div><hr/><i> This is a touch too dark for my taste and I think it''s a stretch for your title.  Is this all you think your darling will be thinking about when she leaves you?  Is this all you want her to think about to convince her to stay?<br/>TC </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>As I said, I tried to find a title to go with this photo of a darling. For me, a darling is a male (since I am female). And this is meant as humor, of course. :o)) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I got it, and obviously liked it!  If this had been of a female torso, you probably would have won, but it''s a sexist world we live in.</td>', 'Originally posted by Louison: Originally posted by TooCool: This is a touch too dark for my taste and I think it''s a stretch for your title.  Is this all you think your darling will be thinking about when she leaves you?  Is this all you want her to think about to convince her to stay?TC   As I said, I tried to find a title to go with this photo of a darling. For me, a darling is a male (since I am female). And this is meant as humor, of course. :o))   I got it, and obviously liked it!  If this had been of a female torso, you probably would have won, but it''s a sexist world we live in.', '2006-06-12 12:36:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2509284, 340223, 40465, '<td valign="top">Hi from CPT2!<br/> <br/>I like the processing on this.  This is a very nice candid shot.  <br/>Congrats on top 20, again:)</td>', 'Hi from CPT2! I like the processing on this.  This is a very nice candid shot.  Congrats on top 20, again:)', '2006-06-12 12:46:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(2509299, 340597, 10047, '<td valign="top">Excellent bokeh! The spot where the honey joins the spoon seems a little bright, but very nice composition overall.</td>', 'Excellent bokeh! The spot where the honey joins the spoon seems a little bright, but very nice composition overall.', '2006-06-12 12:51:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2509468, 340370, 10985, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by ragamuffingirl:</b></div><hr/><i> If this had been of a female torso, you probably would have won, but it''s a sexist world we live in. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I thought something like that, too. More photographers are male than female. I may not have won, but I would have done much better. &lt;sigh&gt;</td>', 'Originally posted by ragamuffingirl: If this had been of a female torso, you probably would have won, but it''s a sexist world we live in.   I thought something like that, too. More photographers are male than female. I may not have won, but I would have done much better. <sigh>', '2006-06-12 13:45:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2509703, 340761, 9668, '<td valign="top">Great job!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-06-12 14:32:17.</i></td>', 'Great job!', '2006-06-12 15:24:29.000000', '2006-06-12 15:32:17.000000', 0),
(2509708, 340761, 9668, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/><b>Composition: </b>I think the composition of this photo is pretty good. There are only a few improvements that I can see. The biggest thing that I notice (which is actually pretty small) is that I would like to have the enter image shifted up slightly. Then the flags at the top wouldn''t be cropped off, and the edge of the crowd at the bottom would be cropped off (so the eye isn''t distracted by that small edge). I like how the cars are almost directly on the lower right rule of third intersection point. Did you consider entering a shot from sometime during the race so one could see the race in action? But maybe the lack of them racing fits your title better, I don''t know.<br/> <br/><b>Technical: </b>My main complaint on the technical side is that the crowd does look slightly blue. A bit of color balancing would have helped this out. I like the clarity of the image though; almost everything in this image is very visible. The sharpness of the image helps with this as well.<br/> <br/><b>Overall: </b>I don''t think the scene is too busy. The starting line is an obvious focal point and the rest of the scene helps move the eye around it. It''s true that there are a lot of elements in this photo, but there is also enough dead space to even it out. This image does an excellent job of showing the overall scene. Having the cars in motion may be another interesting element to consider, but still looks good as it is.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-06-12 14:30:23.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Composition: I think the composition of this photo is pretty good. There are only a few improvements that I can see. The biggest thing that I notice (which is actually pretty small) is that I would like to have the enter image shifted up slightly. Then the flags at the top wouldn''t be cropped off, and the edge of the crowd at the bottom would be cropped off (so the eye isn''t distracted by that small edge). I like how the cars are almost directly on the lower right rule of third intersection point. Did you consider entering a shot from sometime during the race so one could see the race in action? But maybe the lack of them racing fits your title better, I don''t know. Technical: My main complaint on the technical side is that the crowd does look slightly blue. A bit of color balancing would have helped this out. I like the clarity of the image though; almost everything in this image is very visible. The sharpness of the image helps with this as well. Overall: I don''t think the scene is too busy. The starting line is an obvious focal point and the rest of the scene helps move the eye around it. It''s true that there are a lot of elements in this photo, but there is also enough dead space to even it out. This image does an excellent job of showing the overall scene. Having the cars in motion may be another interesting element to consider, but still looks good as it is.', '2006-06-12 15:27:22.000000', '2006-06-12 15:30:23.000000', 0),
(2509737, 340761, 51538, '<td valign="top">I like the geometry of the shot.  Considering the heat and risk to life, limb, and camera, I reckon you got short-changed in the challenge!  But at least you managed to maintain a sense of humour ;-)</td>', 'I like the geometry of the shot.  Considering the heat and risk to life, limb, and camera, I reckon you got short-changed in the challenge!  But at least you managed to maintain a sense of humour ;-)', '2006-06-12 15:38:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2509841, 340538, 55917, '<td valign="top">Thank you Karmat,<br/>I do appreciate your critique. I will take your ideas and work with them...especially the later in the day. Maybe an early evening shot with him laying the grass with some tiny flowers? Just an idea for next time. <br/>I''ve entered the Green II challenge too...once complete could you give my picture your thoughts as well?<br/> <br/>Thanks again :)<br/>And yes Max is quite the character !!<br/>Kim<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by karmat:</b></div><hr/><i> CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>What a cute little dog.  He looks like he would be a real character.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, this shot is lacking a bit.  He is just kinda lying there (as dog''s do), and the eyes tend to go back and forth from the eyes to the feet, back to the eyes, to the feet, etc.  I''m not sure exactly what could fix it, except perhaps shooting from another angle.  The low perspective works well to give us the "eye view" but other than that, there isn''t much in the composition to hold the viewer''s attention.<br/> <br/>Technically, the large shaded area across his hind section hurts.  The dog just sorta blends in, and the harsh shadows on his face obscure some details there.  Maybe using fill flash or waiting until later in the day would help prevent that.  Also, my first impression was that the focus was off, but the more I look at it, I think it is just his furry nature.  Still, he doesn''t look as sharp as the ground around him.<br/> <br/>Overall, I think it is a fairly decent shot, and one that will definitely help you to remember Max..<br/> <br/>karma </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Thank you Karmat,I do appreciate your critique. I will take your ideas and work with them...especially the later in the day. Maybe an early evening shot with him laying the grass with some tiny flowers? Just an idea for next time. I''ve entered the Green II challenge too...once complete could you give my picture your thoughts as well? Thanks again :)And yes Max is quite the character !!Kim Originally posted by karmat: CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat What a cute little dog.  He looks like he would be a real character. Compositionally, this shot is lacking a bit.  He is just kinda lying there (as dog''s do), and the eyes tend to go back and forth from the eyes to the feet, back to the eyes, to the feet, etc.  I''m not sure exactly what could fix it, except perhaps shooting from another angle.  The low perspective works well to give us the "eye view" but other than that, there isn''t much in the composition to hold the viewer''s attention. Technically, the large shaded area across his hind section hurts.  The dog just sorta blends in, and the harsh shadows on his face obscure some details there.  Maybe using fill flash or waiting until later in the day would help prevent that.  Also, my first impression was that the focus was off, but the more I look at it, I think it is just his furry nature.  Still, he doesn''t look as sharp as the ground around him. Overall, I think it is a fairly decent shot, and one that will definitely help you to remember Max.. karma ', '2006-06-12 16:26:16.000000', NULL, 0),
